<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tansen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–<a href="/years/1589">1589</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Miyan Tansen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tansen was an Indian musician and composer, the most celebrated figure of Hindustani classical music. Trained in the dhrupad style that had grown up at the Tomara court of Gwalior, he served Raja Ramchandra of Rewa until about 1562, when he joined the court of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, who counted him among his 'nine jewels'. Many ragas are credited to him, and he is buried in Gwalior.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tansen.jpg" alt="Tansen" width="320" height="590" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tansen_of_Gwalior._(11.8x6.7cm)_Mughal._1585-90._National_Museum,_New_Delhi..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
